for i,d in enumerate(D,1):
 ident=f'ai-round2-day-{i:02}'
 qs={f'AIR2-{i:02}-Q{j}':x for j,x in enumerate(d['questions'],1)}
 w=dict(id=ident,label=f'Pass 2 · Day {i}',week=2,sourceWeek=2,date='',materialTitle=f'AI Pass 2 Day {i}: {d["title"]}',materialUrl=f'ai-review/round2/day-{i:02}.html',expectedWork='90 minutes: 10 recall, 15 worked teaching/repair, 40 independent mixed practice, 20 error review, 5 handoff. Preserve first attempts, confidence, time and help; upload exported notes plus handwritten working.',status='Published',inlineQuestions=[f'{k}: {v["prompt"]}' for k,v in qs.items()],modelLesson=dict(gradingKeys={k:v['answer'] for k,v in qs.items()},methodTags={k:v['skill'] for k,v in qs.items()},skillTag=d['skill'],roundOneEvidence=d['feedback'],repair='Locate the first failed step; classify calculation, misunderstood question, conceptual gap, prerequisite gap, method selection or retrieval. Keep missing/unreadable evidence separate. Give a smaller repair then a fresh unaided retest. Record timing, help and solution exposure separately.'))
 CAT.append(dict(id=f'ai-round2-topic-{i:02}',day=f'AI Pass 2 · Day {i}',title=d['title'],focus=d['feedback'],weeks=[w]))
 p=lambda t:'<p>'+E(t)+'</p>'
 body=f'<nav><a href="index.html">AI Pass 2</a><a href="/">Aleph dashboard and upload</a></nav><p class="eyebrow">AI Pass 2 · Day {i} · 90 minutes</p><h1>{E(d["title"])}</h1>'+p(d['feedback'])
 body+='<div class="box"><h2>How to spend this session</h2><p>Start with ten minutes of closed-notes recall. Use fifteen minutes for the examples or a needed repair, then forty minutes for the five numbered questions. Spend twenty minutes reviewing the first difficult step and five minutes writing tomorrow’s handoff. Stop at ninety minutes; report unfinished work rather than hiding it.</p><p>Questions 1–3 practise today’s focus. Questions 4–5 retrieve earlier ideas. A correct answer after help is useful practice; mark the help so it can be followed by a fresh independent check.</p></div><p role="status" data-save-status>Drafts save on this browser only. Export and upload them to submit.</p>'
 def field(k,prompt,check=None):
  html=f'<article class="practice"><h3>{E(k)}</h3>{p(prompt)}<label>First attempt and later correction (label them separately)<textarea data-response="{k}" placeholder="Before working, record confidence 0–100%; afterwards record minutes. Keep the original attempt when adding a correction."></textarea></label><label>Help used<select data-help="{k}"><option>Not recorded</option><option>Without notes</option><option>Lesson example used</option><option>Hint used</option><option>Worked answer used</option></select></label>'
  if check:html+=f'<details data-check="{k}"><summary>Worked check · open after your attempt</summary>{p(check)}</details>'
  return html+'</article>'
 body+='<section><h2>What can you retrieve before opening the lesson?</h2>'+field(f'AIR2-{i:02}-R0',d['recall'])+'</section>'
 for j,c in enumerate(d['cycles'],1):
  body+=f'<section data-teaching-step><h2>{E(c["question"])}</h2>{p(c["text"])}<div class="worked"><h3>Follow one complete example</h3>{p(c["worked"])}</div>'+field(f'AIR2-{i:02}-W{j}',c['prompt'],c['answer'])+'<div class="box"><h3>If this step was difficult</h3>'+p(c['repair'])+'</div></section>'
 body+='<section><h2>Can you choose and carry out the method yourself?</h2><p>Close the worked examples. For each answer, state the representation you used, the rule you chose and why each important step follows. Predict your confidence before answering. Use your usual Aleph upload card for these five numbered answers; the tutor reviews them against a separate answer key. The two small worked checks above are learning practice and are not part of this score.</p>'
 for k,x in qs.items():body+=field(k,x['prompt'])
 body+='</section><section><h2>How do the pieces fit together?</h2><p>Every problem asks you to make an object you can reason with: a frontier, a game tree, a truth assignment, a path in a network or a probability table. The method then tells you which change is allowed. A final check asks whether the result answers the original question: cheapest route, forced conclusion or normalized probability. Learning the name of a method helps only when you can make and check those steps yourself.</p><pre role="img" aria-label="Problem solving mind map">Question and assumptions\n  → representation: frontier / tree / assignment / network / factor\n  → choose a rule and explain why it fits\n  → carry out one justified step at a time\n  → check cost, bound, truth or normalization\n  → fresh problem without help\n  → delayed recall and unfamiliar transfer</pre><h2>What did the first difficult step reveal?</h2>'
 body+=field(f'AIR2-{i:02}-REVIEW','For each missed or uncertain question: cite the exact first step that failed; distinguish calculation, misunderstood wording, concept, prerequisite, method choice or retrieval. Write a smaller repair and name a fresh problem for a retest. If work was unreadable or uploaded to the wrong card, record an evidence problem instead of claiming a knowledge gap.')
 body+=field(f'AIR2-{i:02}-HANDOFF','What did you solve independently? What needed help? What remains unfinished? State one specific question for your mentor. Propose a fresh closed-notes check two days later and another about a week later; adjust after the result. These are review proposals, not automatically scheduled extra assignments.')
 body+='<p>The next sessions include mixed retrieval questions inside the same ninety-minute budget. If the evidence identifies a narrow gap, repair it rather than rereading the whole subject. Do not count redoing a visible solution as an unseen retest.</p><button data-export type="button">Export answers and study record for upload</button><p>Exporting saves a text file; it does not submit it. In Aleph, open Artificial Intelligence · Pass 2, choose this day’s card and upload the export with your written working. Check the file title and question IDs before submitting.</p><button type="button" onclick="window.print()">Print</button></section><footer><p class="small">Original practice, based on the GATE DA AI topics and the Skycak tutor skill. This set is not a past-paper reproduction. Content references: <a href="https://inst.eecs.berkeley.edu/~cs188/textbook/">Berkeley CS188 textbook</a> and <a href="https://aima.cs.berkeley.edu/">AIMA</a>. Round 1 feedback is an AI assessment of submitted work; delayed independent transfer remains to be checked.</p></footer>'
 (R/f'day-{i:02}.html').write_text(f'<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>AI Pass 2 Day {i} · Aleph</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../lesson.css"><script defer src="../lesson-workspace.js?v=2"></script></head><body><main data-ai-unit="R2-{i:02}">{body}</main></body></html>')
(R/'catalog.json').write_text(json.dumps(CAT,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
(R/'lesson-data.json').write_text(json.dumps(D,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
rows=''.join(f'<tr><td>November {23+i}</td><td><a href="day-{i:02}.html">Day {i}: {E(d["title"])}</a></td><td>5 numbered problems + 2 supported checks</td></tr>' for i,d in enumerate(D,1))
(R/'index.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>AI Pass 2 · Aleph</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../lesson.css"></head><body><main><nav><a href="../index.html">AI Round 1</a><a href="/">Dashboard</a></nav><h1>AI Pass 2: remembering, choosing and explaining</h1><p>November 24–30, 2026. Seven ninety-minute sessions. AI dates remain unchanged; only DSA and DBMS were postponed. The latest Round 1 feedback marks all 21 numbered answers correct. This pass therefore uses fresh variants, delayed recall and mixed method choices rather than assuming a broad conceptual deficit. Corrected submissions are distinct from first-attempt independence.</p><p>Round 1’s variable-elimination history contains an unrelated DBMS upload, later replaced with accepted AI work. That history is a file-selection issue, not evidence of a missing probability prerequisite. Before each submission, check its title and question IDs.</p><table><tr><th>Date</th><th>Session</th><th>Work</th></tr>'+rows+'</table><h2>What will the feedback record?</h2><p>For each numbered answer: the original reasoning, help and confidence, the first failed step if any, an error type, a specific repair and a fresh retest. A lesson upload is evidence of work submitted. A correct supported reconstruction and an independent delayed solution remain different kinds of evidence.</p><p>Questions 4–5 retrieve older topics. The two example checks teach or repair a small step before the independent set. Keep all of this inside the ninety-minute session; report capacity problems before moving work silently.</p><p><a href="../review-plan.html">See the full current review plan</a></p></main></body></html>')
print('Built seven AI Pass 2 sessions, 35 keyed problems and 14 small worked checks.')
